# Does one goal constraint hold? As an ordinary program.
#
# This was `goal.holds`, the last of the three predicates the capability audit decomposed and the one it
# said was *"a loop whose only blocker was a Python closure standing in for a frame node"*. That turned
# out to be exactly right, and better than expected: the closure is not replaced here, it is **gone**. A
# rule written in the closed vocabulary resolves through the ambient context, so `slot_of` inside a frame
# reads that frame's version without this program containing one word about frames. Every `view=` in the
# Python is machinery for a problem the surface does not have.
#
# What it did need was two things that could not be *said*, and both landed before it:
#
#   * comparing two values without crashing on a mismatch  -> `VKIND` and `compare.mf`
#   * telling *not looked at* from *not satisfied*         -> `VKIND` again, which names `UNKNOWN`
#
# Four sorts, and they are four different questions rather than four cases of one:
#
#   link   — is this edge there? (`transitive` makes it *reach* rather than *adjacent*)
#   known  — has this slot been LOOKED at? a knowledge claim, not a world-state one
#   attr   — does the value satisfy the comparison?
#   type   — is the subject a `t`? or, with no subject, is there any `t` under here?

# This node, as the ambient world sees it.
#
# ⚠ **A gap the vocabulary has, found here.** `related` resolves the node it reads *from* and hands back
# the target **unresolved** — correctly, because an edge names an identity. That is invisible for as long
# as the answer is fed to another vocabulary call, since `slot_of` and friends resolve their own subject;
# it becomes visible the moment the answer goes to something that does not, and `is_a` is exactly that:
# it resolves the neighbours it walks but takes the node it is asked about as given. Handing it an
# identity asks about reality while the rule around it reads a frame — the silent hole the mediation
# layer exists to close, one level out.
#
# Spelled as the vocabulary spells it — ask the context for its resolver, call it *by name* — which is
# also what `access.resolved` does from Python, so there is one resolver and no second opinion. It is
# three instructions repeated rather than a ninth member of the closed set, deliberately: **an island is
# created by the second caller**, and today there is one. If a second rule needs *this node, here*, that
# is the moment to make it a member rather than now.
fn here_now(node) -> thing:
    COPY R(result) F(node)
    NATIVE R(r) "resolver"
    JMPNOT R(r) .end
    INVOKE R(result) R(r) node=F(node)
.end:
    CONST R(done) true


# A fact's participant at a POSITION, which is the surface half of `ugm/fact.py`.
#
# Roles are carried by position here, never by a role name, so a reader asks for position 1 rather than
# for `subject`. The label this maps onto is storage and not meaning: when a fact's participants become
# ordered members, this body changes and every caller stays as it is. That is the whole reason the
# indirection exists, and it is the same trade `_STORED_AT` makes on the Python side.
#
# ⚠ The two tables must agree, and nothing structural makes them — see
# `check_THE_SURFACE_AND_PYTHON_AGREE_ABOUT_EVERY_POSITION`, which walks both for every position rather
# than trusting that a two-entry table is too small to drift.
fn participant(f, at) -> thing:
    EQ R(is_one) F(at) 1
    JMPIF R(is_one) .one
    EQ R(is_two) F(at) 2
    JMPIF R(is_two) .two
    CONST R(result) null
    JMP .end
.one:
    INVOKE R(result) related node=F(f) label="subject"
    JMP .end
.two:
    INVOKE R(result) related node=F(f) label="object"
.end:
    CONST R(done) true


# The subject as this world sees it, or nothing when the constraint names none.
#
# Reading it through `related` rather than off the constraint with `GET` is the whole mediation: in
# reality this resolves to itself and costs nothing, and inside a frame it is that frame's version.
fn constraint_subject(c) -> thing:
    INVOKE R(named) participant f=F(c) at=1
    JMPNOT R(named) .none
    INVOKE R(result) here_now node=R(named)
    JMP .end
.none:
    CONST R(result) null
.end:
    CONST R(done) true


# `under` is the region an existential type constraint looks in, and it is a parameter rather than
# something on the constraint: it is the caller's business which world to enumerate, and the same
# constraint is asked of several.
#
# ⚠ The Python has a case this does not: *the subject is not present in this world at all*, answered by
# the view returning nothing. Under `in_frame` that cannot arise — an unmapped node resolves to itself,
# which is the sparse-frame rule — so a named subject always resolves to something and the case is
# unreachable rather than dropped. Stated here because a silently missing branch is how a rewrite goes
# subtly wrong, and because if resolution ever gains a way to say *not here*, this is where it lands.
fn holds(c, under) -> thing:
    ATTR R(sort) F(c) "sort"
    INVOKE R(here) constraint_subject c=F(c)

.by_sort:
    EQ R(is_link) R(sort) "link"
    JMPIF R(is_link) .link
    EQ R(is_known) R(sort) "known"
    JMPIF R(is_known) .known
    EQ R(is_attr) R(sort) "attr"
    JMPIF R(is_attr) .attr
    EQ R(is_type) R(sort) "type"
    JMPIF R(is_type) .type
    CONST R(result) false
    JMP .end

    # **Both sides through the view.** An edge names an identity, so `here`'s targets are the things
    # themselves and the object read through the vocabulary is this frame's version of it — different
    # nodes for the same individual. Comparing them raw reads `b on c` as false one step after it became
    # true, which is how the identity model was arrived at. `relation_at` resolves what it hands back, so
    # comparing against the resolved object is the one comparison that is right in both worlds.
.link:
    INVOKE R(there) participant f=F(c) at=2
    JMPNOT R(there) .no
    ATTR R(label) F(c) "label"
    ATTR R(transitive) F(c) "transitive"
    JMPNOT R(transitive) .adjacent
    NATIVE R(result) "reaches" R(here) R(label) R(there)
    JMP .end
.adjacent:
    INVOKE R(n) relations node=R(here) label=R(label)
    CONST R(i) 0
.each_target:
    LT R(more) R(i) R(n)
    JMPNOT R(more) .no
    INVOKE R(t) relation_at node=R(here) label=R(label) index=R(i)
    EQ R(same) R(t) R(there)
    JMPIF R(same) .yes
    ADD R(i) R(i) 1
    JMP .each_target

    # A knowledge claim: the slot must have been LOOKED at, not hold any particular value. `UNKNOWN` is
    # what an unread slot answers, and until `VKIND` nothing here could tell it from an absent one — the
    # two were the same silence.
.known:
    ATTR R(key) F(c) "key"
    INVOKE R(got) slot_of node=R(here) key=R(key)
    VKIND R(category) R(got)
    EQ R(unknown) R(category) "unknown"
    JMPIF R(unknown) .no
    JMP .yes

    # An unknown slot does not satisfy a value constraint, and does not falsify it either — `undetermined`
    # is where that distinction lives. Here it is simply not satisfied, which keeps this a predicate.
.attr:
    ATTR R(key) F(c) "key"
    INVOKE R(got) slot_of node=R(here) key=R(key)
    VKIND R(category) R(got)
    EQ R(unknown) R(category) "unknown"
    JMPIF R(unknown) .no
    ATTR R(op) F(c) "op"
    JMPIF R(op) .have_op
    CONST R(op) "=="
.have_op:
    ATTR R(want) F(c) "value"
    ATTR R(hi) F(c) "hi"
    INVOKE R(result) compare op=R(op) got=R(got) want=R(want) hi=R(hi)
    JMP .end

    # With a subject, an ordinary cast. Without one, the constraint is EXISTENTIAL — *is there any `t`
    # under here* — and `under` is the region to look in. A type node is not a thing of its own type, so
    # it is excluded: otherwise `there is a car` would be satisfied by the declaration of `car`.
.type:
    ATTR R(want) F(c) "type"
    JMPNOT R(here) .existential
    NATIVE R(result) "is_a" R(here) R(want)
    JMP .end
.existential:
    JMPNOT F(under) .no
    NATIVE R(found) "instances" R(want) F(under)
    INVOKE R(n) relations node=R(found) label="found"
    CONST R(i) 0
.each_instance:
    LT R(more_i) R(i) R(n)
    JMPNOT R(more_i) .none_found
    INVOKE R(inst) relation_at node=R(found) label="found" index=R(i)
    KIND R(k) R(inst)
    EQ R(is_type_node) R(k) "type"
    JMPIF R(is_type_node) .next_instance
    DROP R(found)
    JMP .yes
.next_instance:
    ADD R(i) R(i) 1
    JMP .each_instance
.none_found:
    DROP R(found)
    JMP .no

.yes:
    CONST R(result) true
    JMP .end
.no:
    CONST R(result) false
.end:
    CONST R(done) true
